     "type": "application",
     "title": "Design Your Own Input Diet",
     "content": "Begin by tracking, for three days, every source of unsolicited information you check: email, messaging apps, news sites, and social feeds, along with roughly how many times a day you check each one. Do not change anything yet, only observe. At the end of the three days, sort each source into one of three categories: sources that changed a real decision at least once during the tracking period, sources that were interesting but did not change any decision, and sources that produced anxiety or distraction without providing anything useful at all. Apply selective ignorance to the third category immediately, removing it from your phone or unsubscribing outright, since a source producing no decisions and no benefit is pure cost. For the first category, the sources that matter, choose two fixed times per day to check them, and write an autoresponder or a brief note to frequent contacts explaining the new schedule and how to reach you if something is truly urgent. For the second category, the merely interesting sources, either move them to a single weekly batch, similar to the Friday news session in this lesson's example, or eliminate them for two weeks as a test and note whether you actually miss anything specific enough to name. After two weeks on this schedule, repeat the three-day tracking exercise and compare how many checks you made and how many of them led to an action you could point to. The goal of this exercise is not austerity for its own sake, but building a personal, evidence-based map of which inputs are worth your attention and which were only ever competing for it.",
